{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":168914,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"90286747-dfcc-44e6-b61c-a47a90da017c","total_cost_usd":0.9089364999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":54658,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":12692,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":54658,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":12692,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":54658,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":54658},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":36458,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.036563,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":12692,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":54658,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.8723734999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"playtester\",\n  \"verdict\": \"targets_missed\",\n  \"summary\": \"Every descent in the matrix lands soft with plate to spare, but the 2–4 pass ablation optimum does not exist at any tuning (measured cheapest pass count is 1 in all nine descents, and only 3.1% of 324 grid worlds satisfy it), the pre-staging braking pass the GDD opens reentry with pegs the heat bar at 687 on a 0–100 scale, and the ship as flown has a staged ballistic coefficient of 15.8 kg/m² against the baseline's own assumed 98.5.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ablation_optimum_not_reachable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.optimal_pass_count asserts 3 passes for every band, and the params' cost curve puts the suborbital minimum at 10.97% plate for n=3 against 20.59% for n=1, on the premise that splitting a descent divides the heat as H/n.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The premise is false when flown. Peak heat barely moves with pass count: suborbital empty reads 100 at 1 pass and 114 at 2; high band empty reads 110, 116, 115, 112, 114 across 1, 2, 3, 4 and 7 passes — a 6% spread, and never below the single-pass value in any of the nine descents. Ablation therefore rises monotonically with pass count everywhere: suborbital empty 20.6 → 31.4%, low full hold 53.8 → 69.1 → 72.7%, high full hold 62.9 → 78.1 → 84.2 → 87.3 → 90.7%. cheapest_pass_count is 1 in 9 of 9 descents. The grid says this is not a tuning accident: optimum_in_window is satisfied by 3.1% of 324 worlds, it is the only one of eight targets no top-scoring config hits, and it is what caps best_score at 7/8 — including at ballistic coefficients of 103.4, so raising drag authority alone does not buy it.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"unstaged_braking_pass_not_survivable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"The intended descent opens with coarse braking before staging — shallow passes with the naked hull soaking slow heat while thrust remains to shape the orbit between passes — and shallow unstaged passes are survivable because cargo damage only begins at 100% heat.\",\n      \"measured\": \"A shallow unstaged pass peaks at 687 (suborbital) and 719 (low) on the 0–100 bar: 6.9x and 7.2x the cargo-damage threshold, survivable=false in both. The economy-balancer's own note measures 215 even at unstaged_heat_multiplier 1.0, so the multiplier is not the cause — a Cd 0.45 hull lingers long enough for the 5-second heat bar to reach equilibrium. This one is tuning, not design: 56.5% of grid worlds satisfy unstaged_pass_survivable, and all twelve top configs do, with unstaged peaks of 4 to 66. The nearest survivable neighbour to the flown world keeps gravity 30 and density 3 and only thins the column — scale height 7.2 m, area 3.5, dry mass 60 — measuring 66.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ballistic_coefficient_below_aerobraking_floor\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.5\",\n      \"claim\": \"baseline.json derives its entire drag ladder — 2.9, 15.0 and 111 m/s of delta-v per pass, and the 3.2x staging gain — from a 500 kg ship at reference_area_m2 3.5, giving 98.5 kg/m² staged and 317 unstaged.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The ship actually flown is 80 kg at the same 3.5 m², so the staged figure is 15.8 kg/m² and unstaged 50.8 — 6.2x and 6.2x below the document those numbers came from, and below the ~50 floor the sweep names as the point where the atmosphere stops the ship rather than braking it. The params and the physics document describe two different vehicles. reference_area_m2 = 0.56 reproduces 98.5 at 80 kg; the grid's 0.4 m² column reaches 103.4 and appears in five of the twelve top configs.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"return_leg_has_no_failure_mode\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.5\",\n      \"claim\": \"§4.5 risk 2 is that the difficulty curve inverts — the return leg is supposed to get harder as hauls improve, and §2.3.7 says the value curve and the risk curve rise together.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Nothing in the descent matrix fails. All nine descents report landed=true, soft_landing=true and plate_survives=true, including the worst legal case — a full hold from the high band on a base 100% plate, which costs 62.9% and touches down at 4.58 m/s. Greed is priced but never punished: full hold versus empty moves suborbital ablation 20.6 → 50.2% and peak heat 100 → 154, a real 54% heat penalty, but it does not reach a threshold that costs the player anything. The grid agrees this is comfortable rather than tuned: greed_costs_something is satisfied by 74.4% of worlds.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"band_difficulty_comes_from_mass_not_altitude\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"claim\": \"Each altitude band up is disproportionately hotter to return from; reentry speed and therefore heat scale with the altitude you return from.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Confirmed as a design finding, exactly as the Researcher predicted. ablation.heat_index 100/103/110 matches the measured empty-ship peaks 100/103/110 to the digit, so the params' own index is honest — but that is a 10% spread across all three bands, while a full hold at fixed band moves peak heat 100 → 154 (54%). Full-hold single-pass ablation is 50.2 / 53.8 / 62.9% across suborbital / low / high: the whole three-band climb is worth less than one hold of cargo. The grid satisfies difficulty_rises_with_band in 52.5% of worlds, so it is achievable — but not from band geometry, whose entry speeds are trapped between circular and escape. The levers are the two §4.5 risk 2 already names: cargo mass and band-gated ablation.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"full_hold_soft_landing_confirmed_thin\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"landing.descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.6 under the 5.0 m/s soft line, with descent speed scaling as the square root of mass.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Confirmed. Touchdown is 3.09 m/s empty, 3.90 half hold and 4.58 full hold, identical in all three bands — the sqrt-of-mass law is visible and the claimed 4.6 is right. The margin is 0.42 m/s, 8%, and at g=30 that is 0.42 m of free fall, so any bounce or late chute release is an automatic hard landing. The grid warns this is the second-narrowest target in the design: full_hold_lands_soft is satisfied by only 19.4% of 324 worlds.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"high_band_margin_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"claim\": \"Ascent reaches suborbital with 18.9% of the tank, low with 12.8% and high with 6%, and band 3 is a fuel_tank purchase away.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Confirmed exactly: 13.21 kg (18.9%), 8.99 kg (12.8%) and 4.17 kg (6.0%) remaining. The 6% high-band arrival is below the ~8% the deorbit burn needs, so a base ship that reaches the high band is stranded — which is the GDD's own loss condition working as intended, not a fault. Worth stating plainly because it is the one place the current numbers can kill a player, and it kills them on the way up rather than on the way down. Grid context: bands_reachable is satisfied by 32.4% of worlds and the flown config is inside that third.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"proposed_changes\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"baseline.reentry.reference_area_m2\",\n      \"current\": 3.5,\n      \"proposed\": 0.56,\n      \"reason\": \"Restores the baseline's own 98.5 kg/m² staged ballistic coefficient on the 80 kg ship actually flown, lifting it off the ~50 floor below which the atmosphere stops the ship instead of braking it; it costs a full recomputation of the delta-v-per-pass ladder in baseline.derivation, since every one of those figures scales as Cd*A/m.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"baseline.planet.scale_height_m\",\n      \"current\": 12,\n      \"proposed\": 7.2,\n      \"reason\": \"The nearest grid world to the flown one that survives an unstaged shallow pass (g=30, density 3, area 3.5, mass 60) differs only in this, measuring 66 peak heat against 687, and it keeps sea-level density at 3 so the parachute and the heat calibration do not have to be re-derived; it costs a sharper density gradient, which makes the Researcher's dt = 1/120 s integrator floor mandatory rather than advisory.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.heat_cost_coefficient\",\n      \"current\": 0.00074,\n      \"proposed\": 0,\n      \"reason\": \"Adopts §4.5 risk 1's named fallback — flat per-pass cost — because the heat-dependent term is built on an H/n split the simulator measures as false (peak heat varies 6% across 1 to 7 passes) and only 3.1% of 324 worlds produce an in-window optimum; it costs the elegant U-curve and requires ablation.cost_curve to be regenerated as a straight line.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.fixed_toll_per_pass_pct_by_band\",\n      \"current\": { \"suborbital\": 2, \"low\": 2.3, \"high\": 2.9 },\n      \"proposed\": { \"suborbital\": 6, \"low\": 7, \"high\": 9 },\n      \"reason\": \"With the heat term flat, the per-pass toll is the only anti-feathering lever left, and at 6/7/9 a ten-pass descent costs 60–90% of plate while a committed one costs 6–9; the band gating is also §4.5 risk 2's named lever for a difficulty curve that altitude alone cannot supply, and it costs the multi-pass descent its current near-free status.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.optimal_pass_count\",\n      \"current\": { \"suborbital\": 3, \"low\": 3, \"high\": 3 },\n      \"proposed\": { \"suborbital\": 1, \"low\": 1, \"high\": 1 },\n      \"reason\": \"Stops the params asserting a value the simulator contradicts in all nine descents; this is a truthfulness change, not a fix, and it should be read as the flag that GDD 2.3.1's 'never a single plunge' half of the rule is the part the fallback concedes.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"confidence_notes\": [\n    \"The heat scale is self-referential: 100 is defined as an empty single-pass suborbital descent, so 'one pass is coolest' is partly true by construction. The independent evidence is the spread, not the level — per-pass peak heat moves only 100→114 across 1 to 12 passes and 110→116→115→112→114 across 1 to 7 in the high band. That flatness is a measurement and it is what kills the H/n premise, regardless of where the zero point sits.\",\n    \"The reachable pass counts are non-contiguous in a way that smells like the solver, not the physics: suborbital full hold reports {1, 2, 10} and low empty {1, 2, 12}, with braking_altitude_m clustered at 84–88 m against a 90 m atmosphere top. The interior of the pass-count space may be unexplored rather than unreachable. This weakens the per-pass ablation figures for n>2; it does not weaken the finding, because n=1 versus n=2 is measured directly in every case.\",\n    \"The simulator's pass counter counts atmospheric entries, so a long shallow decaying entry that never leaves the air scores as n=1. The design's 'single plunge' and the sweep's 'n=1' are therefore not guaranteed to be the same trajectory, and the ablation finding should be re-checked against a definition that counts periapsis passages before the fallback is committed.\",\n    \"The 687 unstaged reading is an equilibrium artifact as much as a heat figure — a Cd 0.45 hull spends tens of seconds in the air and the 5-second dissipation constant lets the bar saturate. If the real fix is the heat model's time constant rather than the atmosphere, my scale_height proposal is treating the symptom. GDD 2.3.1 fixes heat_dissipation_s at 5, which is why I did not propose moving it, but a human should decide which of the two is actually wrong.\",\n    \"Nothing in either sweep exercises attitude. off_retrograde_penalty is 1 and the pilot is perfect, so the cosine penalty GDD 2.3.1 specifies is never paid; a real player drifting off retrograde will run hotter than every number above, and the 8% touchdown margin at full hold is the first thing that will eat.\",\n    \"The exploration grid varies only gravity, density, scale height, frontal area and dry mass. It does not vary thrust, tank size, or the ablation coefficients themselves — so the 3.1% satisfaction rate for optimum_in_window is conditional on the params' ablation model being the one scored. It is strong evidence that no *world* produces the optimum, and weaker evidence that no *cost model* does.\",\n    \"Landing, tether shear and the tow fee are arithmetic in this sweep rather than flown dynamics. The 4.58 m/s touchdown is a terminal-velocity solution, not an integrated descent with a chute-opening transient, and the economy-balancer's shear margins (86.1 N against 260) are algebra neither sweep tested. Treat the touchdown finding as confirmed-in-model and the tether numbers as untested by me.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":107122,"ttft_stream_ms":1765,"time_to_request_ms":451,"type":"result","duration_ms":166701,"uuid":"723619c4-9ab5-4075-bfdd-08bea8fa53c6"}
